<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="Theme.Body" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Primary brand color. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/glass_tint</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryVariant">@color/glass_base</item>
        <item name="colorOnPrimary">@color/white</item>
        <!-- Secondary brand color. -->
        <item name="colorSecondary">@color/glass_accent</item>
        <item name="colorSecondaryVariant">@color/glass_accent</item>
        <item name="colorOnSecondary">@color/black</item>
        <!-- Status bar color. -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/glass_base</item>
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
    </style>

    <!--
      three full-screen Activity(HtmlActivity / LookActivity / ScanActivity)shared.
      inherit Theme.Body gets colorPrimaryVariant as the status-bar colour, just a purple bar;
      and DayNight light windowBackground makes every open flash white once before darkening.
      both are app the rest(pure black + #7FA5FF, see ui/Glass.kt)unrelated, so here
      all pinned.windowBackground is Glass background colour: overlay card, dialogs and this
      the three full-screen faces share one palette, that first frame shouldn't be an exception.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Body.Fullscreen" parent="Theme.Body">
        <!-- DarkActionBar hands every one of these an "Andee" title bar over
             the brain's content, and on the camera screens it covered the ✕
             pill outright. HtmlActivity used to hide it in code; doing it here
             means a new full-screen face can't forget. -->
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">#FF000000</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      three full-screen faces(HtmlActivity / LookActivity / ScanActivity)the entrance now shared
      and the exit, see ui/Stage.kt.

      windowAnimationStyle="@null" the only new item here, also"flash once when turned off"
      the real reason: nobody on the inheritance chain has touched this item, so the platform, at finish() after that, as usual
      play its ownExitanimation —— the animation's content is no longer on screen, what fades out is just an
      empty window; and underneath overlay the card is onDestroy only then starts to recover. The two
      so the two motions are offset, that middle frame is neither one, that is the flash the user sees.

      change to"fade in while scaling"and"fade out while shrinking"by StageActivity draw itself, the platform's
      this item only needs to shut up.enter/exit all kept 0, because enter the animation's
      onCreate only starts after, the platform has already drawn its frame —— keep 0 gives it no chance at all.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Body.Stage" parent="Theme.Body.Fullscreen">
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      launcher icon(SelfCheckActivity)only. When the service is running, that icon means
      "Open app": along the way Activity not draw a single pixel, expand() and then finish ——
      see SelfCheckActivity.onCreate. But the platform, at Activity before the first draw it first presses
      windowBackground lay down a starting window, so it feels like"tap the icon → screen
      goes black once → the card grows in", but the long-press on the ball doesn't do that. Turn it off, the entrance of both routes is what
      the same thing.

      harmless even with accessibility off: that route is pure black to begin with + revealStage from 0.94
      grows in, whether the first frame is black or empty makes no difference, and the card's first frame is SelfCheck.local synchronise
      give, no black screen waiting for the network.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Body.Stage.NoPreview" parent="Theme.Body.Stage">
        <item name="android:windowDisablePreview">true</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      LaunchPadActivity only: not draw a step or leave a frame. The Stage's job is to take
      Service sent by intent in"foreground"hand off to the target in app —— hand it off and immediately finish,
      so the whole window must be transparent and not steal focus during the animation: windowIsTranslucent make it invisible,
      windowDisablePreview + windowAnimationStyle make the first frame and the animation both nonexistent.
      backgroundDimEnabled turn off, otherwise some ROM will, when transparent Activity with a layer underneath
      dimming curtain, target app before it comes up the screen flashes dark once.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Body.Transparent" parent="Theme.Body.Stage.NoPreview">
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:backgroundDimEnabled">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
